From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 08:01:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ports/arkweb: Use a disk-backed HTTP cache and persist its
 index on pause

Two fixes and the device test that found them.

The port set `Opts::config_dir` but never `http_cache_dir`, so
`open_default_store()` returned `None` and the webview ran with a memory-only HTTP
cache. That is the opposite of what this embedding wants: a web engine hosted
inside someone else's application is exactly where keeping cached bodies out of
the process matters. It now uses `<cacheDir>/web/servo/http-cache`, with the cache
directory from the application context. ArkWeb keeps its own data in
`<cacheDir>/web`, which the system's cache trimming spares, and Servo stands in for
the system webview here. The port's config directory is no guide: ACE passes the
application's base directory as `--user-data-dir`, outside `cacheDir`.

`ServoNWeb::OnPause` -- which ACE already calls on window hide and host-application
background, and which the port already implements for throttling -- now also calls
`NetworkManager::notify_application_backgrounded()`, so the cache index reaches
disk before the host can be frozen and killed. `NWeb::OnPause` is pure virtual, so
the webview always receives this; no host cooperation is required. The bridge call
waits for the flush rather than enqueuing it, bounded by a 500 ms timeout so a busy
Servo thread cannot stall the ACE UI thread.

`NWeb::NotifyMemoryLevel` is the other lifecycle hook worth wiring here. It is
still a no-op stub, and was stubbed precisely because Servo had no cache-trim API;
the embedder API added by patch 121 is the natural place for it.

`test_http_cache_index.py` is the regression guard, end to end on a device: start
from an empty cache directory, load a cacheable page, send the app to the home
screen, assert the index reaches disk, force-stop (the kill an OS reaper performs
on a frozen application), restart, and assert the index was *loaded* rather than
rebuilt.

That last assertion is the point. A purely functional check cannot tell the two
apart -- a rebuild finds the same entries, so the page is served from cache either
way and the test would pass with the flush removed -- so it asserts the
`http-cache: index loaded from disk` marker and the absence of
`index rebuilt by scanning`. It also asserts `index-data` does *not* exist before
the home key, without which the test could be observing the 20 s debounce rather
than the background flush. The cache directory is located with `find` rather than
hard-coded, so a layout change fails loudly instead of quietly asserting nothing,
and it must be under `cache/web/`.

Fork note: from the `http-cache-v2` branch, unchanged.

Validation: on the http-cache-v2 branch, `HDC_TARGET=<serial> uv run ports/arkweb/tools/test_http_cache_index.py` passed 3/3 on the DAYU200 (~20 s), against a shim deployed with `deploy.py`; it must run through the PEP 723 script header, since bare `pytest` does not resolve `hdc-py`. Negative control by hand: deleting `index-data` before the restart makes the log show only `index rebuilt by scanning`. On this stack, with the shim built from patches 120 to 123 including their review fixes: passed 3/3 on the DAYU200 (OpenHarmony 7.0), with the cache in `/data/app/el2/100/base/<bundle>/cache/web/servo/http-cache`; the build before that change put it in `<bundle>/servo/http-cache`, which the test now rejects, and `./mach check --ohos -- -p arkweb` clean. After a rebase, re-run the device test: it is the only coverage of `OnPause` calling the flush and of the port setting `http_cache_dir`.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent wrote this on the http-cache-v2 branch, including the device test, and ported it)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
